text:
LONDON, March 26 (Reuters) - Ukraine can "scrape through" in 1998 and maintain the intervention band for the hryvnia currency, but it will be a close call, Credit Suisse First Boston said on Thursday. 

"It will be a tough challenge for the government and the central bank to ensure an adequate flow of funding," the investment bank said in a note. 

In particular, a breakdown in the International Monetary Fund programme for the country would raise the risk of reserves falling back to the rock-bottom levels seen in late January and early February. 

"Given the expectation that the parliamentary elections on March 29 will be won by the leftist forces, the risk of a breakdown of the IMF programme cannot be entirely ignored, despite pressure from the U.S. government on the IMF to keep the Ukraine afloat." 

But even though the risk in Ukraine is substantially higher than in Russia, CSFB said current yields of 49 percent for Ukrainian treasury bills were attractive, if investors were not liable to a yield tax of 30 percent. 

At present it is unclear whether non-residents' interest earnings on T-bills are subject to the tax. 

CSFB calculates that gross outflows in 1998 will be $4.9 billion, comprising the current account deficit, principal payments on foreign currency debt, maturing local currency debt held by foreigners, and leads and lags in settlements for imports and exports. 

So far, Ukraine has secured funding inflows of $1.125 billion for 1998, comprising $875 million of bond issuance, $150 million from a private placement and $100 million received from the IMF. 

CSFB said other potential sources of funding in 1998 included: 

- a further eurobond and a first samurai issue, both in May/June ($1 billion); 

- the rollover of at least one third of the current foreign holdings of T-bills into 1999 ($600 million); 

- survival of the current IMF Standby programme ($400 million); 

- further World Bank disbursements ($525 million); 

- European Bank for Reconstruction and Development loans ($200 million); 

- downpayment on investment in the motor industry ($350 million); 

- possible bilateral loans from the United States and the European Union ($400 million); 

- non-payment for Russian gas ($300 million). 

If the balance of payment flows pan out as expected, the hryvnia is unlikely to depreciate by more than the limit of the currency band for 1998, which is 2.25 to the dollar, CSFB said. The unit was last trading at just under 2.04 to the dollar. 

((Ben Hirschler, London newsroom +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)). 

(C) Reuters Limited 1998.

